`sSup` invariant (domination,\\n14: annihilation, the zero-forcing family, and mathlib's own `indepNum`/`cliqueNum`).\\n15: \\n16: That blocks the cheapest source of formal results we have. The refutation engine\\n17: kills thousands of candidates and knows exactly which graph kills each one; each\\n18: such pair is a `¬∀ …` theorem whose proof is \\\"evaluate both sides on this graph\\\"\\n19: — but only if the invariants actually evaluate.\\n20: \\n21: The definitions here are the same mathematical objects expressed as a minimum\\n22: over an explicitly enumerated `Finset`, which the kernel *can* reduce. They are\\n23: deliberately kept in their own namespace: nothing in the existing proof pipeline\\n24: changes, and `GraphInvariants.lean` remains the specification of record.\\n25: -/\\n26: import Mathlib\\n27: \\n<truncated 4868 bytes>\\n────────────────── -/\\n121: \\n122: /-- Sum of the `k` smallest degrees.\\n123: \\n124: Stated as a minimum over `k`-element subsets rather than by sorting the degree\\n125: sequence: the two agree (a `k`-subset of minimum degree-sum is exactly a choice\\n126: of `k` smallest degrees), and this form stays decidable, whereas both\\n127: `Multiset.sort` and `Finset.toList` are noncomputable and stall kernel\\n128: reduction. -/\\n129: def smallestDegreeSum (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (k : ℕ) : ℕ :=\\n130:   (((univ.powersetCard k).image (fun s => ∑ v ∈ s, G.degree v)).min).getD 0\\n131: \\n132: /-- Sum of the `k` largest degrees (dual of `smallestDegreeSum`). -/\\n133: def largestDegreeSum (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (k : ℕ) : ℕ :=\\n134:   (((univ.powersetCard k).image (fun s => ∑ v ∈ s, G.degree v)).max).getD 0\\n135: \\n136: /-- Annihilation number: largest `k` whose `k` smallest degrees sum to `≤ m`. -/\\n137: def annihilationNumber (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ :=\\n138:   maxUpTo (Fintype.card V) (fun k => smallestDegreeSum G k ≤ size G)\\n139: \\n140: /-- Slater number: least `t` wit